WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:09.772
At the lending desk, a clerk picks up each item a member wants to borrow, looks at it once, and stamps a due date.

2
00:00:09.772 --> 00:00:11.871
A large-print book gets six weeks.

3
00:00:11.871 --> 00:00:16.131
A new release gets one week, because other people are waiting for it.

4
00:00:16.131 --> 00:00:17.799
A box set of DVDs gets two.

5
00:00:17.799 --> 00:00:22.244
The clerk works down a printed sheet, and the first line that fits wins.

6
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:26.017
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:26.017 --> 00:00:29.089
An instanceof chain and a switch do the same job.

8
00:00:29.089 --> 00:00:31.345
Why should I care which one I write?

9
00:00:32.467 --> 00:00:33.891
Here's the old way.

10
00:00:33.891 --> 00:00:34.865
Is it a Book?

11
00:00:34.865 --> 00:00:36.589
Then describe the book.

12
00:00:36.589 --> 00:00:38.013
Else, is it a Disc?

13
00:00:38.013 --> 00:00:39.288
Else, a Magazine?

14
00:00:39.288 --> 00:00:40.187
Else, throw.

15
00:00:40.187 --> 00:00:44.609
And here's the same sorting as a switch, one case per kind.

16
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.427
Each line uses a pattern: a test with a name attached.

17
00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:50.307
Book b asks: is this a Book?

18
00:00:50.307 --> 00:00:53.666
If it is, you get b, already a Book, with no cast.

19
00:00:53.666 --> 00:00:56.688
And both versions print the same three lines.

20
00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.553
So they look like twins.

21
00:00:59.553 --> 00:01:00.794
Let's break them.

22
00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:03.350
Delete the Magazine line from both.

23
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:04.665
Which one notices?

24
00:01:04.665 --> 00:01:05.833
Pause and guess.

25
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:10.576
The chain still compiles.

26
00:01:10.576 --> 00:01:17.074
The run reaches the magazine, falls into the last else, and dies with an IllegalStateException.

27
00:01:17.074 --> 00:01:19.399
The switch doesn't compile at all.

28
00:01:19.399 --> 00:01:25.896
javac, the Java compiler, says: the switch expression does not cover all possible input values.

29
00:01:26.067 --> 00:01:27.925
Look at the chain's last line.

30
00:01:27.925 --> 00:01:30.526
It says unreachable, with a question mark.

31
00:01:30.526 --> 00:01:34.119
Code that asks itself questions is usually right to worry.

32
00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:39.384
So the switch is checked against the sealed list, and the chain is checked by nobody.

33
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:46.906
In the desk picture, the switch is a clerk whose printed sheet lists every kind of item the library has.

34
00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:49.778
The chain is a clerk who remembers the kinds.

35
00:01:49.778 --> 00:01:50.225
Mostly.

36
00:01:50.225 --> 00:01:54.885
Kotlin's when, over a sealed type, is checked the same way as the switch.

37
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.286
A switch can do more than check the kind.

38
00:01:58.286 --> 00:02:01.258
A record pattern reads several boxes in one look.

39
00:02:01.258 --> 00:02:04.229
Book, then in brackets, var format and var isNew.

40
00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:10.717
If it's a Book, the case takes out its format and whether it's a new release, right there in the case line.

41
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.740
Now the clerk's rules, from the GuardsDemo.

42
00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.515
Line one: a large-print book, 42 days.

43
00:02:17.515 --> 00:02:19.852
Line two: a new release, 7 days.

44
00:02:19.852 --> 00:02:22.116
Line three: any other book, 21.

45
00:02:22.116 --> 00:02:27.228
The part after when is a guard, an extra condition the case must pass.

46
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.122
Here comes a new book, in large print.

47
00:02:30.122 --> 00:02:32.915
It fits line one, and it fits line two.

48
00:02:32.915 --> 00:02:33.918
How many days?

49
00:02:33.918 --> 00:02:35.064
Pause and guess.

50
00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:38.676
42 days.

51
00:02:38.676 --> 00:02:42.868
Cases are tried from the top, and the first match wins.

52
00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:46.907
Now swap lines one and two, and the same book gets 7.

53
00:02:46.907 --> 00:02:48.888
javac accepts both orders.

54
00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:52.531
javac can't read your mind about which rule matters more.

55
00:02:52.531 --> 00:02:58.608
Only a test that sends a new large-print book through the switch can tell you which order you meant.

56
00:02:58.608 --> 00:03:02.740
The sheet is read from the top, so put the most specific rule first.

57
00:03:02.933 --> 00:03:10.779
Guards don't count toward covering every case, so each family of guarded lines ends with a plain one, like line three.

58
00:03:10.779 --> 00:03:19.289
And if you move line three to the top, javac refuses the lines below it: this case label is dominated by a preceding case label.

59
00:03:19.289 --> 00:03:21.151
They could never be reached.

60
00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:23.395
Kotlin writes guards with if.

61
00:03:23.395 --> 00:03:25.884
But kotlinc has no dominance check.

62
00:03:25.884 --> 00:03:30.150
Put the plain Book first, and it compiles without a warning.

63
00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.856
Every book gets 21 days.

64
00:03:31.856 --> 00:03:34.700
Here kotlinc shrugs where javac objects.

65
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.599
Here's where the picture breaks.

66
00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:43.285
The lesson's experiment compiles a switch over a member's account status: active or closed.

67
00:03:43.285 --> 00:03:48.783
Then only the list is rebuilt, with a third status, frozen, and the old switch meets it.

68
00:03:48.933 --> 00:03:51.249
A real clerk would ask a colleague.

69
00:03:51.249 --> 00:03:53.102
The switch can't ask anyone.

70
00:03:53.102 --> 00:03:57.204
javac added a hidden last arm, and it throws a MatchException.

71
00:03:57.204 --> 00:03:59.520
Loud, which beats guessing quietly.

72
00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.833
So, three guardrails.

73
00:04:01.833 --> 00:04:07.771
Exhaustive means exhaustive for the list javac saw, so recompile when the list changes.

74
00:04:07.771 --> 00:04:10.023
Put the most specific rule first.

75
00:04:10.023 --> 00:04:13.163
And end each guarded family with a plain case.

76
00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:16.035
So why care which one you write?

77
00:04:16.035 --> 00:04:18.494
The chain is a clerk with a good memory.

78
00:04:18.494 --> 00:04:26.673
The switch is a clerk with a printed sheet, checked by the compiler: every kind covered, every line reachable, and read from the top.

79
00:04:26.867 --> 00:04:27.637
Quick check.

80
00:04:27.637 --> 00:04:32.063
The order is now: new release first, then large print, then any book.

81
00:04:32.063 --> 00:04:35.015
How many days does a new large-print book get?

82
00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:38.409
7.

83
00:04:38.409 --> 00:04:41.613
The new-release line comes first, so it wins.

84
00:04:41.613 --> 00:04:46.811
javac won't warn you that the large-print line is shadowed for new books.

85
00:04:46.811 --> 00:04:48.021
Only a test will.
